{
  "id": "GP04Z",
  "version": "2.0",
  "title": "Upper endoscopy (EGD)",
  "description": "Upper endoscopy, also called EGD (esophagogastroduodenoscopy), is a procedure where your doctor uses a flexible camera to examine your upper digestive tract. Your doctor looks at your esophagus (food tube), stomach, and the beginning of your small intestine to diagnose or treat problems. Before the procedure, your doctor will numb your throat with a spray to minimize gagging. You'll be sedated to keep you comfortable while your doctor gently advances the camera down your food tube. During the procedure, your doctor can perform various treatments depending on what they find. The procedure typically takes 10-20 minutes, though therapeutic procedures may take longer. The cost of this procedure may vary depending on the complexity involved. Categories include diagnostic, therapeutic, simple, therapeutic, with eus, ercp variants (sphincterotomy, stent, stone extraction), and transmural drainage (pseudocyst).",
